<p class="hint center">For planning only. Results depend on the figures you enter and your policy’s terms. They aren’t a quote, an appraisal or legal advice.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Why split the loss?</h2>
<p>Standard homeowner policies exclude flood, including storm surge. Wind and wind-driven rain damage may be covered by the homeowner policy, while rising water is covered, if at all, by a separate flood policy. Organizing the loss by cause before you meet either adjuster helps each claim go to the right place.</p>
<h2>How it works</h2>
<ul>
<li>Tag each item as wind, flood or mixed. For mixed items, enter the share you attribute to wind.</li>
<li>The homeowner portion uses the hurricane deductible if you choose one, otherwise your standard deductible.</li>
<li>The flood portion uses the flood policy deductible you enter.</li>
</ul>
<h3>A caution</h3>
<p>This is an organizing tool, not a coverage decision. Insurers and adjusters decide causation, and disputes about the cause of damage can fall outside appraisal. Flood policy limits and contents coverage are not modeled. Figures are illustrative.</p><h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who decides whether damage was caused by wind or flood?<svg class="ic chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Each insurer investigates causation under its own policy. If the two disagree, a Rhode Island attorney can explain your options.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does the National Flood Insurance Program have appraisal?<svg class="ic chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>NFIP policies contain their own appraisal provision with specific rules, separate from your homeowner policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why is the hurricane deductible so large here?<svg class="ic chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Because it’s a percentage of dwelling coverage, not of the loss. Use the hurricane deductible calculator to see the dollar figure.</p></div></details></div><h2>More free tools</h2><div class="related"><a href="/policyholder-tools/settlement-gap-estimator/"><small>Tool</small><span>Settlement Gap Estimator</span><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" st